WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.100
Bad Street Brawler, Thug-Thrashing Defender of Freedom.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:26.780
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.480
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:29.620
And I'm Joe.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:32.980
You know, the title is kind of unclear here.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:37.460
Are you fighting on Bad Street, or are you just bad at street brawling?

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.220
I think it's Bad Street.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:48.180
Like, you know, like Skid Row, whatever the other names for violent neighborhoods could be.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.700
Yeah, this is Bad Street.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.800
See, I'm not even doing a bit here.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.460
I actually thought it was something different than either of those things.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:59.440
I thought that you were a street brawler, but you're like a bad street brawler.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:00.480
Like, you're like a bad dude.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:02.620
Oh, okay. Like bad dudes.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:03.420
Like a bad enough dude.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.040
Yeah. Yeah, you're a bad enough dude to brawl on the streets.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:17.080
Anyway, you know, Bad Street Brawler is a beat-em-up, and I feel like that's all you really need to know in terms of like overview for this game.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.360
But there is a lot of substance in the manual.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:24.600
It's not communicated too well in the actual gameplay of it because it's just a basic arcade beat-em-up.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:31.070
But the manual sets up like this guy Duke that you play as, and he has a whole reason for being on the streets, taking on these...

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:33.390
I don't remember the name of the gang.

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:36.570
Do either of you? No, I don't.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:39.030
I just know that he wants to clean up the streets.

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:43.490
Yeah, he does want to clean up the streets, but there's like two sides of this for Bad Street Brawler.

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:48.030
There's like the guy who is invested in the manual and this blonde-haired dude named Duke.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:02:01.530
And then there's like the game and you know, like how who in 2022, like you're not the person who's getting the whole package of buying the game or looking at it in the toy store first and being like, man, I want to be like this guy, right?

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:05.210
You know, I want to take care of business and beat all the bad guys off the street.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:07.150
You're just playing this game.

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:08.250
You're just starting it up.

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:10.210
You're, you know, you're greeted with the menu.

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:25.430
This is like the one like menu screen or just intro screen that I had never played the game, but I just like it's in my head as one of like the most iconic like NES screens ever for some reason.

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:31.630
Like that, that not really cool dude that's supposed to look cool.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:36.830
I feel like I see that everywhere when talking about retro games.

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:39.950
Like is this like a cult hit?

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.810
This guy looks like Neil Patrick Harris, right?

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:47.470
And that's not somebody that I think is going to do like a great job cleaning up the streets.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:49.490
I just appreciate that.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:58.850
He's like he's looking pretty like, oh, don't don't mess with me or don't mess with don't don't lead a life of crime.

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:02.170
But he's also just wearing a vest and short shorts.

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:03.910
And it's very funny.

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:05.810
I don't know. I wasn't sure.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:13.530
First of all, this was intentionally funny because I kind of came off as it was, but I've never seen King of the Hill.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:19.850
Yeah, but this guy gives me at least on the menu screen because the real King of the Hill vibes.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:39.610
So I feel like before we get into the game, because we probably never looked at the story, we owe it to everybody to just set up who is Duke Davis and what he's all about.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.950
So in the manual, this is what they give you as the story for the game.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:47.330
Law breaking crooks, thugs and circus renegades rule the mean streets.

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:49.190
And, you know, just go pause here for a second.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:54.330
That's some kind of streets where, you know, there's crooks, there's thugs and also a circus is happening.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:57.490
But here we are, and it's a real zoo out there.

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:00.970
The city is unsafe and the good citizens live in constant fear.

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:06.210
You are Duke Davis, former punk rocker and the world's coolest martial arts vigilante.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:13.130
You are a lean, mean, thug thrashing machine returning to your native city streets as the ultimate defender of freedom.

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:17.730
There's evil waiting behind every bush and danger lurking behind every corner.

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:23.530
Look out, it's up to you to send the bad guys reeling with furious fist and foot bashing action.

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:26.050
It's nonstop martial arts madness.

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:30.030
Nail the no gooders today and watch the street savages scatter.

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:34.450
I don't know about you guys, but I just read the whole thing and I feel like that could have been three sentences.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:37.110
Yeah, you're fighting bad guys.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:38.110
Yeah, one sentence.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:39.370
There you go, Joe, you did it.

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:48.790
Is it just, I feel like it's all just there to justify that they have all these assets for just circus people.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:53.770
And they didn't know how to fit it into why you would be fighting them.

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:57.090
Yeah, they were working on a Ringling, Barnum and Bailey circus game.

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:58.550
And that just got canceled.

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:01.530
So they had to work it into their beat them up game, which happened to be this.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:08.030
But you know what's funny is like now that I've read the story, I kind of realized like, oh, it didn't matter whether you read the story or just play the game.

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:09.270
You get the idea.

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:19.610
Yeah, I'm thinking that maybe like, like even this, this paragraph, maybe I'm too optimistic because I've grown up in this time period where we parody things more.

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:22.430
But I like looking at this as an intentional parody.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:25.590
And that makes it really actually kind of funny to me.

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:27.370
But I'm sure it wasn't.

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:29.610
You know, how does this fare as a beat them up?

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:32.570
Like, we don't need to get into the specifics of anything.

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:34.290
What stands out about this game?

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:36.070
What works for this game?

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:38.510
Like the most generic of levels for you guys?

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:45.110
What's going on here? I think if it's as far as something that works for this game, I'll start with that because I have plenty of other things.

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:50.370
I will say a good thing about this game that I noticed right away is like the fluid movement.

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:52.550
Now, that's not the controls.

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:55.850
I think there are a problem with the controls, but the actual motion.

00:05:55.850 --> 00:06:09.750
I don't know why so many other games in this type of genre, not even just beat them up, but like the side scroller, even like platformers can't really nail the like smoothness of just walking that that doesn't feel really stiff.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:15.430
And I thought that that actually was a right off the bat, probably the first thing I noticed something that felt good about this game.

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:31.970
That's true. But it also sort of like it, it doesn't have that typical that heads up display, but like the, the, it doesn't give you anything to tell you like, okay, it's a good, it's cool to move, move forward.

00:06:32.150 --> 00:06:34.350
Now it's like, there's going to be enemies that come.

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:38.970
And I think after a while you'll thin them out so that the only thing that you can do is move to the right.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:45.410
But like, it, it doesn't really communicate that well, like what's locking you from, from continuing to move forward.

00:06:46.450 --> 00:06:55.070
Um, and you could just like spend all of the seconds, all of the precious seconds that you have, like pretty early in, in the game.

00:06:55.110 --> 00:07:00.190
And so you're not even testing out how, how fluid the walking feels or something like that.

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:08.850
Sorry, I'm getting kind of deep into this one little thing too early, but I, I just felt like that was one of the main, uh, things that I hung on.

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:10.230
I'm getting into playing this game.

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:15.150
It's true though, Sean, cause it's like two different forms of progress in each of these stages for a beat them up, right?

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:19.130
Like there's the timer that is constantly ticking away as you play through the level.

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:25.390
But then there's also the moments where you're like locked to the screen versus, you know, the moments that you can just proceed on with.

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:28.450
Like, I'm not going to deal with this guy because time is also very valuable.

00:07:28.670 --> 00:07:31.230
You don't have a way to gain more time.

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:34.030
So you do have to try to beat these levels as fast as possible.

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:37.010
I don't know if they ever give you more than like 100 seconds.

00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:42.410
I admittedly didn't beat the game, but 100 seconds is not a lot of time when you're trying to take care of enemies.

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:46.490
So it's really like a balance in terms of, you know, what we know about beat them ups.

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:53.570
Cause beat them ups are usually either you must defeat every enemy on screen or you must find a certain thing to then like get the progression to move forward.

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:58.330
This one chooses a mixture of time and scrolling to indicate how far you're moving.

00:07:58.330 --> 00:07:59.810
They have that on the UI.

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:02.130
These like what I'm going to say are just like foam boots.

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:03.850
I think they're buildings. Yeah.

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:06.370
And they, they tell you how far in the stage you are.

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:14.450
But, um, you know, I don't know if this is like the best indicator because like, what is the measure of, you know, like one building, one screen?

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:16.870
Is that like, you know, is one building, two screens?

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:18.430
How do you know? Yeah.

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:21.090
How much more distance do you have to go compared to time?

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:24.550
I didn't find myself finding that either useful or referring to it that much.

00:08:25.630 --> 00:08:30.850
Yeah. I referred to it just in the sense of like, how, like, am I getting close?

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:33.470
You know, seeing, okay, I only got like two things left.

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:38.110
I know that like, I don't have to manage my time so closely because I'm really close to the end.

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:49.810
But I, I, yeah, I'm assuming that just like somewhere in the games programming, it's just set that like when you get to this point in the stage, the game, the screen will lock and a boss will come in.

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:52.530
And yeah, there's no way for you, the player to know that.

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:55.130
So you just kind of got to deal with it when it happens.

00:08:56.090 --> 00:09:01.090
Yeah. Cause I've had, you know, the muscle memory of playing other beat-em-ups.

00:09:01.210 --> 00:09:05.530
And so I was approaching it in the way of just like, Oh, if there's an enemy, you've got to defeat it.

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:10.830
Um, and that ended up with me like, you know, running out of time all the time.

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:15.430
So then I had to just train myself to just always be testing the right bound.

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:25.690
Um, and, uh, like, so I ended up playing on the right side of the screen, most of the game, um, just so that I didn't waste that much time.

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:33.490
Because like, I think I, I, I mean, I got beat up quite a bit, but most of my deaths are just from running out of time.

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:43.150
I just say, I found that, um, that I would just literally run past everyone until the game stopped me and I had to fight someone in order to progress.

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:45.470
Like it took me a while to figure that out.

00:09:45.590 --> 00:09:49.910
But once I started doing that, that was like, those are like my best levels.

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:51.670
Yeah. Duke is a pretty powerful guy.

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:54.250
You know, you take damage just like how you would in any other game.

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:56.710
Like where if you get attacked, it takes the health point away from you.

00:09:56.810 --> 00:10:03.950
But with the exception of some projectile enemies or some enemies that are a bit more aggressive with just the way that they move around the screen.

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:10.110
For the most part, you know, any enemy that just enters stage right or stage left, they're not that hard to take care of.

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:12.790
And there's not, you know, the screen is never littered with enemies.

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:14.270
So I kind of appreciate that.

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:25.850
But it is confusing to know that, you know, like when you're in the middle of like a, you know, a mid stage boss fight, where, you know, you're locked between you and this other guy who the enemy has like a little more health than usual.

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:30.310
And that's shown in the UI, but it's like hard to just, what's the point of the stage?

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:35.050
Like at any given time, it just feels kind of, you know, random, even though it's not.

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:44.190
It's completely like that layout of the stage that I don't know, it was tripping me up a little bit that I kept moving between boss fights, regular enemies, enemies that I could skip.

00:10:44.530 --> 00:10:47.670
There was no real thought process there of what I was doing.

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:50.850
Yeah, it's definitely a grab bag.

00:10:50.850 --> 00:11:06.370
Like, and I get that, like, you know, you can, the designer can throw a different assortment at you that, like, obviously the circus dwarves, I don't know what the correct way to say it is.

00:11:07.110 --> 00:11:09.970
Like, you're going to have to use your low attack on them.

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:17.890
You're going to want to use a, like your more heavy attack on the gorillas and stuff like that.

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:22.910
Like, I get that they want to keep you off kilter and you have to like sort of change your style depending on who you're fighting.

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:29.790
But yeah, I don't think that there was any real thought process into like what combination you were going to get.

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:38.750
Because you're also like, I think it might be random depending on how much you just decide to blow through until you get, you get stopped again.

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:43.610
So it doesn't really allow them to really tailor that experience.

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:46.910
And another thing that changes constantly between levels is your move set.

00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:52.270
We haven't really seen something like this before and I think they tried to make this an interesting hook of the game.

00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:55.970
But I don't know if it really benefited them in the way that they might have imagined it.

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:59.950
You know, they probably thought this will give you some variety, right?

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:05.750
Because you're every level, you have a different move set and you have to figure out what you'll do with that move set.

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:07.410
But it always, it's always the same.

00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:11.630
It's either just either A to attack, B attack or A and B together attack.

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:14.970
And then, you know, like, you know, you're locked to that specific stage.

00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:18.830
Those are your moves for that level and they call them force moves for whatever reason.

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:22.570
And there are 15 of them, but you can only use the three of them per stage.

00:12:22.690 --> 00:12:26.930
And I feel like it would have been better off to have some kind of version of inputs with the D pad.

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:30.370
And you'll learn a new force move each level or something.

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:36.150
So they continue to build upon these things instead of just, I hope you really like this move because it's the last time you're ever going to do it.

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:45.990
Yeah. I initially thought like, yeah, this is kind of cool because it makes you think about it forces you to think about each level a little differently.

00:12:46.390 --> 00:12:50.850
Not, you know, not that all the moves are necessarily like super different from each other, but some really are.

00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:53.430
And at first I was like, oh, that's kind of neat.

00:12:53.690 --> 00:13:08.610
But, but it did very quickly get old that like, well, you know, it felt like I should have been gaining some progress and like, yeah, gaining these moves or even like, I mean, this is now just me putting together like a wish list of, you know, maybe this would just be a different game.

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:13.750
But like, you know, even if, if there was just, Hey, here's the combination from level one, here's the combination from level two.

00:13:14.050 --> 00:13:17.650
Now you can, you can pause it and select one of those combinations.

00:13:17.650 --> 00:13:27.450
So it's still a little challenging because you, you can't ever use, you know, the roundhouse kick with the trip move because they're on different sets or something.

00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:31.890
So you still have those like that way where you have to strategize in that sense.

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:40.990
But like, you feel like you progressed and you gained these moves and you can, I don't know, it feels too arbitrary that it just throwing something else at you.

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:47.270
Like, I like the idea of it, but I think something else needed to be done in order to, to make it an interesting, like fun thing.

00:13:47.450 --> 00:13:49.070
Well, to sidestep real quick.

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:54.270
One thing I want to point out is that I really do like the presentation of, of how this works.

00:13:54.270 --> 00:14:05.450
Like between each level you get like into a little training room and you can experiment with the, the, the moves you're going to use in the next level on a punching bag.

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:18.430
And I, I, I just appreciate that little, like here, get your bearings time and how that's sort of, it's almost a thing to look forward to because I did like, I, it did kind of drive me to get to the next level.

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:20.670
Cause I'm like, Oh, what will the next moves be?

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:23.650
Like I want to see if they're, if they look cool or if they feel good.

00:14:24.170 --> 00:14:32.770
Um, so I liked that part, but when it comes to like how it slices it up and you only get these three moves for each level.

00:14:32.990 --> 00:14:39.250
Um, and sometimes like, you know, you get, you'll, you'll get like the trip again, or you'll get the ear twist again.

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:48.930
Um, one thing I think they could have done is just have like one of, have, have one of the lighter, because they do have different damage outputs.

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:55.190
Let's have one of the lighter attacks be like a short press and have a heavy attack be a long press.

00:14:55.430 --> 00:15:03.710
And, um, and then you could maybe throw in the directional stuff, like maybe stay away from up because that's jump, but everything else is fair game.

00:15:03.710 --> 00:15:08.730
Um, and they probably could have fit most of these onto the controller by the end of the game.

00:15:09.290 --> 00:15:11.810
Um, but yeah, you're right.

00:15:11.870 --> 00:15:18.890
Like the way it is now, like there really can't be any strategy because you, you don't have all the pieces on the board at once.

00:15:18.890 --> 00:15:28.870
Yeah. And I think it does add some kind of variety just in the types of moves you do, but there really isn't, if you think about it, any difference between the level where you can punch versus the level where you headbutt.

00:15:28.990 --> 00:15:32.850
Like it's doing the same exact amount of damage and you know where it hurts.

00:15:33.190 --> 00:15:34.910
The enemy is still like the same placement.

00:15:34.910 --> 00:15:36.610
So it just looks different, right?

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:45.730
No, I noticed a few like differences in that stuff, which I like, which is where I thought this could, this has potential to be kind of unique and interesting.

00:15:46.070 --> 00:15:54.310
But like, you know, there's a trip move that like, yeah, other moves might be like a low sweeping kick or just a low kick.

00:15:54.610 --> 00:15:59.950
But that like the trip move, like you can kind of like hold the guy down for a minute and you like knock him down.

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:03.110
Whereas like the low kick just does damage low or something like that.

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:04.310
That could be not exactly right.

00:16:04.310 --> 00:16:15.150
Some of them are grapples that like don't actually do damage, just repositions them that I find kind of useless in a game where time is so much not on your side.

00:16:15.430 --> 00:16:18.490
But that is still like differences.

00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:30.210
Right. And the other, the other one, the one that I loved, it was my favorite was the headbutt or like the charge one where you lower your head and charge because it is, first of all, a means to continue moving forward while attacking.

00:16:30.210 --> 00:16:32.530
So I could just literally ignore everyone.

00:16:32.710 --> 00:16:36.630
And if somebody happened to come out of the right side of the screen, I would be headbutting them already.

00:16:37.530 --> 00:16:39.270
Yeah. It's like Sonic spin dash.

00:16:39.910 --> 00:16:42.210
Yeah. So like, and that was fun.

00:16:42.310 --> 00:16:50.790
Although I don't know what was going on, but I, you know, every time I tried this, any level where I had that would just glitch the heck out.

00:16:50.930 --> 00:16:55.610
Like just visually, I could still, I could still play it, but like everything was, was messed up.

00:16:55.610 --> 00:16:59.550
Like, I don't know if you guys experienced that because, because, you know, I would restart the game.

00:17:00.530 --> 00:17:03.330
Not that it needed, made me restart the game, but restart the game and try it again.

00:17:03.770 --> 00:17:07.410
Um, and every time I got to a level like that, it would just get like super glitchy.

00:17:07.910 --> 00:17:09.390
Huh. I didn't like abuse it.

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:12.250
So I guess I don't know if that, you know, if that has something to do with it.

00:17:12.370 --> 00:17:13.270
Oh, I abused it.

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:31.430
Yeah. This game has a very unique setting and atmosphere and a variety of enemies.

00:17:31.830 --> 00:17:37.390
I feel like regardless of the execution of what moves you use, these are some of the most creative enemies since Stinger.

00:17:37.630 --> 00:17:41.450
Remember, like Stinger had watermelons and saxophones and stuff.

00:17:41.710 --> 00:17:48.070
Like it's not, it's not that wacky, but you know, in the same way, it's the, the enemies, you know how they fight.

00:17:48.070 --> 00:17:58.930
Like you got the basketball player who will throw basketballs at you and you've got, uh, you know, there's this one I'm thinking of where it's the spy who then opens his coat to reveal another smaller spy.

00:17:59.170 --> 00:18:02.330
Like these things are, you know, there, there's a charm to that.

00:18:02.610 --> 00:18:17.630
And even in the backdrop of the city, like being so run down and then the subway and the parts where it almost looks like the wild west, you know, even though there's no one design scheme here, it is, you know, a little strange that the circus just kind of pulled into town the same day the city went to shit.

00:18:17.630 --> 00:18:18.730
It was kind of funny.

00:18:19.010 --> 00:18:20.110
Uh, it just works.

00:18:20.290 --> 00:18:24.310
And I was always entertained by who was going to come out next or where you were going to go.

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:29.430
There's like this Ric Flair wrestler character who comes around and he does somersaults around the screen.

00:18:30.070 --> 00:18:32.110
Like, I don't know, all that stuff was just very charming.

00:18:32.590 --> 00:18:33.490
Attila the honey.

00:18:34.250 --> 00:18:36.150
Tell me about Attila the honey, Sean.

00:18:36.850 --> 00:18:40.650
I mean, it's just, it's just like the token, the token female enemy.

00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:43.490
And I just appreciate that that's the name in the manual.

00:18:46.590 --> 00:18:47.630
Yeah. Cause you know what?

00:18:47.790 --> 00:18:54.230
Before I was like, Oh, I'm sure this is the reason I think it's trying to be funny is just because I'm more used to the game parody.

00:18:54.370 --> 00:18:56.550
No, this game definitely knows it's funny, right?

00:18:56.670 --> 00:19:00.670
Yeah. Now that we're talking about this stuff, it's absolutely like trying to have a sense of humor.

00:19:00.790 --> 00:19:01.790
And I appreciate that.

00:19:01.950 --> 00:19:07.670
The game knows it's funny because this is the second you beat the first stage, you start seeing quotes between levels.

00:19:07.670 --> 00:19:14.730
That's when you know that they're in on the joke because I think, you know, there's just a few that I made note of, you know, come fight beside me.

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:19.890
I said to myself, and although it doesn't make sense, I held my own hand as a small sign of trust.

00:19:20.070 --> 00:19:21.790
And together I made my defense.

00:19:22.230 --> 00:19:24.070
Like what? What is he saying?

00:19:24.450 --> 00:19:29.850
When trouble troubles you, don't, wait, don't trouble trouble until trouble troubles you.

00:19:29.990 --> 00:19:32.270
But that is like the full quote from Duke in the game.

00:19:32.390 --> 00:19:33.810
And there's more things like that too.

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:39.150
Every time he cleans up the street, right, he then like gets celebrated via MS Paint Art with the rest of the city.

00:19:39.670 --> 00:19:43.770
What? You know, they're like celebrating his good fortune.

00:19:43.770 --> 00:19:47.690
So there's one where he's like getting his hand raised by the mayor or something.

00:19:47.730 --> 00:19:50.070
And he just says, I'll have a cheeseburger.

00:19:50.330 --> 00:19:51.150
It's like, what?

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:52.990
Like in regards to what?

00:19:53.150 --> 00:19:55.130
We never, we're not in on the joke.

00:19:55.310 --> 00:19:59.270
And, you know, that Duke's in on the joke, but we still have to laugh a little bit because that's funny.

00:19:59.570 --> 00:20:02.150
He goes, maybe they'll make a movie out of me.

00:20:02.290 --> 00:20:03.370
I think that's kind of funny.

00:20:03.370 --> 00:20:08.190
The developers are like, hey, if you're willing to pay for a movie, we will license Bad Street Brawler to you.

00:20:08.450 --> 00:20:13.330
Yeah. The MS Paint comment, though, cracks me up because I'm looking at one of these right now and you're right.

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:17.750
This was confirmed in my mind to have been made in Microsoft Paint.

00:20:18.830 --> 00:20:20.190
That's exactly what it looks like.

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:22.130
Yeah, there's something off-putting about that.

00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:28.230
Yeah. The visuals are very strange because the actual game doesn't look like it's made in MS Paint by any means.

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:31.850
There's some, you know, nice pixel artwork going on in here.

00:20:31.990 --> 00:20:38.410
But then there's just these moments of full screen still images that can only be done with, you know, like the paint bucket tool.

00:20:39.070 --> 00:20:44.350
Yeah. Well, it's funny because on the manual, he looks like Neil Patrick Harris.

00:20:44.350 --> 00:20:47.450
Because then in the gameplay, I think he looks like an old man.

00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:50.650
You know, he looks like, you know, to go back to last week, he looks like old Biff.

00:20:50.650 --> 00:20:59.290
And then in these other screens, he looks like, I don't know, like some weird, like Johnny Bravo Microsoft Paint.

00:20:59.350 --> 00:21:02.170
Like there's no consistency as to what this character looks like.

00:21:02.310 --> 00:21:15.270
I also just appreciate that when like you, when you clear a level and you throw all the weapons into the dumpster, like the bananas from Gorillaz also qualify as cleaning up a street of weapons.

00:21:15.630 --> 00:21:17.790
So it's got some good stuff.

00:21:17.790 --> 00:21:20.390
Yeah. I mean, that is great though.

00:21:21.130 --> 00:21:22.730
Like thematically, it all works.

00:21:22.890 --> 00:21:26.930
And I feel like, you know, they worked in the idea of the weapons that you collect throughout the stages.

00:21:27.170 --> 00:21:32.530
Yeah. You throw them in the dumpster as if like that's how we as a society get rid of guns.

00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:36.370
It's like we just throw them all in the trash and agree not to go back into the trash.

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:39.350
Clap your hands. Yeah. My job here is done.

00:21:40.370 --> 00:21:41.270
Yeah. It's like, right.

00:21:41.490 --> 00:21:43.530
They're gone now. But you know what annoyed me about that?

00:21:43.530 --> 00:21:51.990
Even though it's like, so it's such a cute idea to have this guy who was like cleaning the streets single handedly and, you know, removing all the weapons from them.

00:21:51.990 --> 00:21:57.690
And when he throws the weapons into the trash, the points come out as numbers like 400, 600, right?

00:21:58.050 --> 00:22:02.850
But then when you start, you know, doing this, when they hit the floor, they bounce like chattering teeth.

00:22:03.070 --> 00:22:05.010
I don't get what they were going for there.

00:22:05.170 --> 00:22:08.530
You know, those Toy Story chattering teeth things like you wind them up.

00:22:08.530 --> 00:22:10.550
I don't know why the 400 is doing that.

00:22:10.830 --> 00:22:11.730
Little things like that.

00:22:12.170 --> 00:22:15.070
You know, the game had some thematic fun flair going for it.

00:22:15.150 --> 00:22:17.110
And then it also just has over the top silliness.

00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:20.470
I don't know. I thought that that was not even that silly.

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:23.290
It just looked like, oh, they put some physics on the numbers.

00:22:23.770 --> 00:22:24.910
I thought that was fun.

00:22:25.530 --> 00:22:28.050
Yeah. Physics on the numbers is not silly, Joe?

00:22:28.210 --> 00:22:30.930
Well, it's silly, but it's not sillier than the rest of the stuff.

00:22:31.070 --> 00:22:35.750
A lot of times when you see like a title or something, they put it like in a movie or something.

00:22:35.750 --> 00:22:37.430
They'll put it like in the environment.

00:22:37.670 --> 00:22:39.450
They'll put it like in 3D space.

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:40.770
And this just gives it a little bounce.

00:22:40.970 --> 00:22:42.750
I don't know. It's not that silly to me.

00:22:43.010 --> 00:22:44.530
It just gives it a little bounce.

00:22:45.010 --> 00:22:49.010
Yeah. Other ones float up and they move side to side rather than just appearing on the screen.

00:22:49.170 --> 00:22:51.150
It just gives, I don't know, it just makes it look like a thing.

00:22:52.330 --> 00:22:55.410
The more I think of Duke's whole thing, I'm enjoying this game a lot more.

00:22:55.410 --> 00:22:59.470
Just thinking about the little quest that he set himself out onto to get back to his hometown

00:22:59.470 --> 00:23:01.190
and clean the streets up.

00:23:01.190 --> 00:23:05.650
You know, he said he's grabbing people, he's lifting them up over his head, and he's just

00:23:05.650 --> 00:23:07.310
shaking them until they lose all their health.

00:23:08.230 --> 00:23:10.190
I mean, that's what I would do.

00:23:10.690 --> 00:23:17.890
It's pretty much, he's really like a neighborhood, a friendly neighborhood Spider-Man.

00:23:18.050 --> 00:23:20.170
Yeah. Without the wall climbing.

00:23:20.450 --> 00:23:22.570
Well, you don't, well, we didn't finish the game.

00:23:22.570 --> 00:23:27.630
Did anyone beat the game? Yeah, that might be the B move in one of the late levels.

00:23:29.290 --> 00:23:34.550
Okay, the end of the game reveals that the guy behind it all, the end boss, is Duke Sensei,

00:23:34.710 --> 00:23:38.330
the man who brought him up and taught him how to be the ultimate martial artist.

00:23:38.770 --> 00:23:42.890
So now you must take out Duke Sensei, who is just really like, if you thought Duke looked

00:23:42.890 --> 00:23:46.310
old, this dude is like, you know, he's like a grandfather to somebody.

00:23:46.510 --> 00:23:51.330
He is old, and he clearly thinks that he, you know, he must have taught Duke well, because,

00:23:51.730 --> 00:23:55.570
you know, if Duke's trained in the martial arts, he's coming equipped with a bazooka.

00:23:57.470 --> 00:24:01.110
He's like, I might have taught you the ways of the fist, but I have a bazooka and I'm

00:24:01.110 --> 00:24:02.030
prepared to use it.

00:24:02.130 --> 00:24:04.210
And I just feel like that's a weird ending as it is.

00:24:04.330 --> 00:24:09.650
But then, you know, rather than get some awesome quote or some kind of ending like epilogue

00:24:09.650 --> 00:24:15.130
where Duke reunites with his high school sweetheart or something, instead, it just shows him in

00:24:15.130 --> 00:24:19.470
the, you know, like a car with confetti that doesn't fall, but does animate in the same exact

00:24:19.470 --> 00:24:20.650
place over and over again.

00:24:20.650 --> 00:24:22.410
And he's just cheered on by people.

00:24:22.950 --> 00:24:29.650
This looks like, this looks like, what's that comic book with Jeremy and...

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:36.590
It does look like that, but I don't remember what that's called, Sean, so we'll just have

00:24:36.590 --> 00:24:38.410
to try to find that and I'll link it in the show notes.

00:24:38.910 --> 00:24:41.930
But no one's going to know what we're talking about because we don't even know what it's

00:24:41.930 --> 00:24:44.430
called. I don't know what you're talking about either.

00:24:44.430 --> 00:24:48.450
But it's a comic book and Sean is definitely right that it looks just like that.

00:24:49.290 --> 00:24:53.410
But it's also just like a weird, okay, the city held a parade for him, I suppose, but

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:56.590
they were also doing a bunch of other celebratory things for him between levels.

00:24:56.730 --> 00:24:59.670
I just feel like the ending should have had, you know, been a little bigger.

00:25:00.210 --> 00:25:04.150
Like, now you've cleaned the streets are clean and here's how many weapons you took off the

00:25:04.150 --> 00:25:05.230
streets this playthrough.

00:25:05.750 --> 00:25:08.530
Something, you know, like that would have given a little more to the game.

00:25:09.050 --> 00:25:11.530
Like, now nobody does drugs in this town anymore.

00:25:11.670 --> 00:25:14.170
Something like that. Would have been a good ending message.

00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:16.130
Not that drugs are bad.

00:25:18.010 --> 00:25:19.350
In the 80s, they sure were.

00:25:19.730 --> 00:25:21.550
Yes, in the 80s, they had bad drugs.

00:25:22.030 --> 00:25:23.690
Oh boy, I shouldn't have said that.

00:25:27.130 --> 00:25:29.090
Oh, sweet bro and hella Jeff.

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:47.970
God damn it. You know, the NES version of this game was only one of two NES games designed to be used

00:25:47.970 --> 00:25:49.230
with Mattel's power glove.

00:25:49.230 --> 00:25:54.250
And unlike our attempts to try to locate a power pad to play the power pad games, I didn't

00:25:54.250 --> 00:25:58.530
even think about trying to acquire a power glove and play this game because I just genuinely

00:25:58.530 --> 00:26:00.390
can't think about like how that would work.

00:26:00.770 --> 00:26:01.830
Why this game?

00:26:02.250 --> 00:26:03.810
With the way the power glove works?

00:26:04.270 --> 00:26:07.950
The way I understand it and the way this game worked doesn't make sense.

00:26:07.950 --> 00:26:10.370
It seems like I missed out on any potential features.

00:26:10.790 --> 00:26:13.070
I still don't actually know.

00:26:13.070 --> 00:26:22.550
I know that it's a controller on your hand and it's got some design, but do you move it?

00:26:23.590 --> 00:26:26.590
Like there's inputs or is it just buttons on your hand?

00:26:26.750 --> 00:26:28.690
Right. So it has like the whole keyboard on it.

00:26:28.790 --> 00:26:33.870
Right. But then like you were supposed to be able to punch in the code for the particular game

00:26:33.870 --> 00:26:37.090
and then the power glove would work based on that particular game.

00:26:37.670 --> 00:26:41.370
So you had to you had to know the code of the game and then it would change, you know,

00:26:41.450 --> 00:26:43.510
and obviously, you know, it's like a Mattel thing.

00:26:43.630 --> 00:26:47.250
So it wasn't other developers weren't telling them, you know, here's the code to put with

00:26:47.250 --> 00:26:49.730
that. And then, you know, that's how the power was supposed to work.

00:26:50.010 --> 00:26:51.590
You had to just, you know, figure it out.

00:26:51.590 --> 00:26:57.010
And I believe the way it works is some kind of motion from your fingers, you know, that

00:26:57.010 --> 00:26:58.250
like work with the glove.

00:26:58.910 --> 00:27:00.190
So, but we're not sure.

00:27:00.410 --> 00:27:02.870
Well, no. I feel like we need to get one.

00:27:03.310 --> 00:27:07.350
Yeah. Well, I guess what I'm saying is like, that's how it's supposed to work and it just didn't,

00:27:07.450 --> 00:27:09.450
you know? Oh, okay.

00:27:10.150 --> 00:27:15.270
Right. So like you had to set it up and it kind of worked like how VR, some, some VR headsets

00:27:15.270 --> 00:27:21.150
work now where you, you have to like actually set up a receiver and, you know, like a stand

00:27:21.150 --> 00:27:25.730
that sets up that you know where the glove is in the sensor of the zone of the receivers.

00:27:26.110 --> 00:27:27.570
And then, yeah, it's like that.

00:27:27.790 --> 00:27:31.310
Like, uh, you know, it had like a spatial awareness or something.

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:37.890
I don't, I don't really know how tech works with that, but it had an idea where your hand

00:27:37.890 --> 00:27:39.330
was in relation to the sensors.

00:27:39.330 --> 00:27:40.890
And so you're supposed to be able to move.

00:27:41.110 --> 00:27:42.790
So it's some form of motion sensing.

00:27:42.950 --> 00:27:47.530
Right. You were supposed to be able to like move the glove to move, but also like, you know, per

00:27:47.530 --> 00:27:48.430
finger. Yeah.

00:27:48.490 --> 00:27:50.230
That's why it has like that bar around the knuckles.

00:27:50.390 --> 00:27:51.390
It has another bar.

00:27:51.510 --> 00:27:53.690
It was supposed to be able to like detect finger movements as inputs.

00:27:54.090 --> 00:27:57.770
So like my index finger could be, you know, I don't know, I might be making this up,

00:27:57.830 --> 00:27:59.670
but like my thumb could be the A button.

00:27:59.890 --> 00:28:01.810
Okay. That seems awful.

00:28:02.210 --> 00:28:06.430
It does seem awful, but I still think it's like, it's pretty sweet looking.

00:28:06.870 --> 00:28:10.630
Oh yeah. Now the other game that we will have to play super glove ball.

00:28:10.890 --> 00:28:14.270
Now that sounds like a game that you might need the power glove to be useful.

00:28:14.670 --> 00:28:20.030
Some power pad games were only ever compatible, like with the power pad, you know, otherwise

00:28:20.030 --> 00:28:24.670
you had to really force yourself to either emulate it and use a keyboard to pretend like

00:28:24.670 --> 00:28:26.490
you're standing on the power pad with your fingers.

00:28:26.830 --> 00:28:30.690
You know, the power glove doesn't require that, but super glove ball, maybe it's some kind

00:28:30.690 --> 00:28:33.430
of bowling game where you need to use your fingers and stuff like that.

00:28:33.950 --> 00:28:38.990
But Bad Street Brawler just seems like a weird attempt as a starting game for the power glove.

00:28:39.350 --> 00:28:45.710
Yeah. Cause it's, it seems so, uh, primarily designed to just be played on a controller.

00:28:45.710 --> 00:28:51.530
Like I don't understand what would be beneficial about this glove thing.

00:28:51.990 --> 00:28:55.530
Yeah. It's just because it's like, it's like a side scrolling thing anyways.

00:28:55.770 --> 00:29:01.410
So like your movements wouldn't really translate to like the perspective of the character.

00:29:01.970 --> 00:29:04.290
And I couldn't find, you know, it's definitely not in the manual.

00:29:04.510 --> 00:29:08.470
I couldn't find any information about this, but allegedly the standout feature of why you

00:29:08.470 --> 00:29:12.470
would want to use Bad Street Brawler's path with the power glove is that there's some

00:29:12.470 --> 00:29:15.410
moves that can only be done with the glove, but I'm just not sure.

00:29:16.050 --> 00:29:20.170
If the manual doesn't even cover it, let alone the game, the game doesn't ever tell you that

00:29:20.170 --> 00:29:23.730
it's time, right? The game never says like, make sure you have your power glove on.

00:29:23.950 --> 00:29:25.990
Yeah. I didn't know this was a power glove game.

00:29:26.210 --> 00:29:28.010
Yeah. I mean, I, I, I read it.

00:29:28.110 --> 00:29:30.690
I read it on Wikipedia before playing it.

00:29:30.690 --> 00:29:36.530
Um, but I mean, is it, is it just like you get, is it just like a pay to win by having

00:29:36.530 --> 00:29:38.970
the power glove? Is it just that easy with the glove?

00:29:39.150 --> 00:29:40.950
Like, are they that good of the special?

00:29:41.190 --> 00:29:42.830
I don't know. Yeah.

00:29:42.870 --> 00:29:45.970
You punch harder and then like now every enemy is one hit KO.

00:29:46.330 --> 00:29:51.290
Yeah. And you know, looking at it now, real time followup, the glove allows you to do a special

00:29:51.290 --> 00:29:56.250
move where if you push the glove forward towards the screen, you do a special move once per

00:29:56.250 --> 00:29:57.730
stage called the glove zap.

00:29:57.970 --> 00:30:00.330
This automatically defeats one enemy.

00:30:00.690 --> 00:30:06.350
This can also be done pressing left and right together on the NES controller, which of course

00:30:06.350 --> 00:30:10.430
makes this move impossible to perform on a standard controller and making it exclusive

00:30:10.430 --> 00:30:11.490
to the power glove.

00:30:24.090 --> 00:30:28.290
On the sequels and spinoff side, originally Bad Street Brawler was released for home computers

00:30:28.290 --> 00:30:29.530
as Bop and Rumble.

00:30:29.530 --> 00:30:30.870
That sounds terrible.

00:30:31.390 --> 00:30:33.170
Or in Europe as street hassle.

00:30:33.550 --> 00:30:35.310
Now I'd get street hustle.

00:30:35.870 --> 00:30:37.950
Street hustle kind of works.

00:30:38.190 --> 00:30:41.410
Street hassle? It's a hassle to be in those streets.

00:30:41.770 --> 00:30:43.290
Yeah. You're hassling people.

00:30:43.770 --> 00:30:47.030
Yeah. Maybe it's just a British like stiff upper lip thing.

00:30:47.150 --> 00:30:52.090
Like, oh, it's such a hassle living in this wretched hive of a city.

00:30:52.850 --> 00:30:56.870
I don't know. I do think Bad Street Brawler comes out on top here.

00:30:57.110 --> 00:30:58.070
Yeah, I think so too.

00:30:59.270 --> 00:31:01.430
All right. Now here's a weird piece of trivia for you, Sean.

00:31:01.730 --> 00:31:02.670
Are you ready for this one?

00:31:02.810 --> 00:31:05.690
Okay. Last week, Back to the Future episode, okay?

00:31:05.970 --> 00:31:09.010
We mentioned Back to the Future Part 2 and 3 on the NES.

00:31:09.010 --> 00:31:12.070
Is there going to be a Bad Street Brawler Part 2 and 3?

00:31:12.350 --> 00:31:13.610
No, but if you remember, we couldn't.

00:31:13.810 --> 00:31:19.290
At that moment, you were the LJN expert and we couldn't figure out if LJN made Back to the Future Part 2 and 3.

00:31:19.630 --> 00:31:22.690
I said, you know, I'm just taking a guess here, but I don't think they did.

00:31:22.690 --> 00:31:30.390
Well, it turns out that the developers of Bad Street Brawler, Beam Software, will be the ones who make Back to the Future Part 2 and 3.

00:31:30.590 --> 00:31:40.310
Whoa. Now, what are the odds that a game that gets released right alongside another game in a movie franchise then gets to make the next sequel to the said game that came before it?

00:31:40.590 --> 00:31:41.530
Like, I don't know.

00:31:41.630 --> 00:31:43.090
Like, that just feels very strange.

00:31:43.390 --> 00:31:48.950
Like, that's got to be, you know, the odds have to be almost impossible for something like that to ever happen again, right?

00:31:48.950 --> 00:31:53.750
